    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> power ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusa">Hattusa</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> that rivalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> for control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#levant">Levant</a>.</li><li>Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hittite-vassal-treaties">vassal treaties</a> and its peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> are among the earliest milestones of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#international-law">international law</a> and diplomacy.</li><li>It vanished in the Bronze Age Collapse when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusa">Hattusa</a> was abandoned and burned c. 1180 BCE; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#neo-hittite-states">Neo-Hittite</a> city-states carried on its legacy.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>A replica of the Hittite-Egyptian peace treaty, the earliest to survive in both parties' versions, hangs at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-nations">United Nations</a> headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-york">New York</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

