    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hattusili-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hattusili-i.jpg" alt="Hattusili I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Korahettita_id%C5%91szak_t%C3%A9rk%C3%A9pe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA; colorized and wording by Laszlovszky András at Hungarian Wikipedia based on Világtörténet tíz kötetben, Kossuth..., Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hittites established themselves on the Anatolian plateau during the early second millennium BCE, absorbing the indigenous Hattian population whose religious traditions and royal titles they largely inherited. The Old Kingdom emerged around 1650 BCE under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusili-i">Hattusili I</a>, who made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusa">Hattusa</a> his capital, a naturally defensible site among the rocky highlands of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>, and campaigned southward into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a>. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mursili-i">Mursili I</a> carried out the dynasty's most audacious early venture, raiding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a> around 1595 BCE and ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#old-babylonian-empire">First Babylonian Dynasty</a> in a campaign of extraordinary operational reach that nonetheless produced no permanent territorial gain. A subsequent period of palace murders and coups, recounted in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#edict-of-telipinu">Edict of Telipinu</a> around 1525 BCE, revealed the structural fragility of early Hittite kingship. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-hittite-kingdom">New Hittite Kingdom</a> reached full imperial maturity under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a> (c. 1344–1322 BCE), who transformed the Hittites into a genuine great power competing directly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mitanni">Mitanni</a> for control of the Levantine corridor. His dismantling of the Mitanni kingdom and installation of Hittite-aligned rulers across Syria, including his own sons at Carchemish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aleppo">Aleppo</a>, established the imperial framework that defined the empire at its height.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hittite economy rested on the agriculture of the Anatolian plateau, supplemented by herding and the exploitation of substantial metal deposits, particularly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>. The Hittites occupied a strategically vital position astride the routes connecting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aegean">Aegean</a>, and merchants operating under Hittite protection took part in the long-distance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tin">tin</a> and copper trade that supplied the ancient world's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze">bronze industry</a>. Land was formally held by the palace and the temples and distributed to warriors, officials and cultivators in return for military service or labour obligations, an arrangement documented in detailed land-grant texts. The state stored and redistributed grain through royal magazines, providing a buffer against the harvest failures to which the plateau was prone. Tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a> supplemented agricultural revenues significantly, making the empire's fiscal health directly dependent on its peripheral vassal network. The loss of Syrian vassals to Egyptian or Assyrian competition therefore meant not merely territorial contraction but immediate fiscal damage.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kadesh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kadesh.jpg" alt="Battle of Kadesh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Egypt_Abou_Simbel6.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hittite military was built around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chariot">chariot</a> as its primary offensive arm, deploying heavier three-man vehicles carrying a driver, a shield-bearer and a spearman, in contrast to the lighter two-man Egyptian designs. This configuration prioritised shock and close combat over mobile archery. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-kadesh">Battle of Kadesh</a> in 1274 BCE, fought against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#orontes-river">Orontes River</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#muwatalli-ii">Muwatalli II</a>, is often described as the largest chariot engagement of antiquity, with perhaps five thousand chariots involved on both sides. Neither side achieved decisive victory, though both claimed triumph, and Kadesh itself remained in Hittite hands. Hittite infantry combined levies and professional palace troops with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> contingents, providing numerical depth but variable reliability. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suppiluliuma-i">Suppiluliuma I</a>'s Syrian campaigns demonstrated sophisticated operational capacity, combining direct military action with diplomatic subversion to detach enemy vassals before engaging their principals. The Hittites conducted systematic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-warfare">siege warfare</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battering-ram">battering rams</a> and siege works described in the Old Hittite account of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#siege-of-urshu">siege of Urshu</a>, though their preferred method remained political isolation rather than costly direct assault.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#hittite-laws"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hittite-laws.jpg" alt="Hittite laws" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#hittite-laws">Hittite laws</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hittite_Cuneiform_Tablet-_Legal_Deposition(%3F).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mx. Granger, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hittite political system centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-king">Great King</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusa">Hattusa</a>, whose authority was simultaneously military, judicial and religious. Unlike the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pharaoh">pharaoh</a>, the Hittite king was not a living god but the earthly steward of the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tarhunt">Tarhun</a>, becoming divine only after death, which created a more negotiated relationship between royal and priestly authority. The empire managed its periphery through a tiered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> system, with subordinate rulers bound by formal treaties specifying military obligations, tribute and diplomatic restrictions. These <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hittite-vassal-treaties">vassal treaties</a>, surviving in substantial numbers from the Hattusa archives, constitute one of the ancient world's most developed bodies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#international-law">international law</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hittite-laws">Hittite legal code</a>, preserved on two tablets, was notably less brutal than contemporary Mesopotamian equivalents, favouring compensation and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#repatriation-of-cultural-property">restitution</a> over mutilation and death. Members of the royal family held key provincial positions, concentrating power within the dynasty but creating recurring succession tensions that periodically destabilised the central government.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#karnak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#karnak">Karnak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">René Hourdry, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-kadesh">Treaty of Kadesh</a>, concluded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusili-iii">Hattusili III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ramesses-ii">Ramesses II</a> around 1259 BCE, is the earliest peace treaty to survive in the versions of both parties: in Akkadian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cuneiform">cuneiform</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusa">Hattusa</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#karnak">Karnak</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ramesseum">Ramesseum</a>. A replica hangs today at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-nations">United Nations</a> headquarters in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-york">New York</a>. The diplomatic correspondence found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#akhetaten">Amarna</a> and Hattusa documents sophisticated great-power relations among the Hittites, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#babylon">Babylon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mitanni">Mitanni</a>, conducted in Akkadian as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> international language. Anatolian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#iron-technology">ironworking</a> contributed to the gradual diffusion of iron technology across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#near-east">Near East</a>. Religious and literary traditions absorbed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattians">Hattians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hurrians">Hurrians</a> were transmitted westward, and the Hurro-Hittite Kumarbi cycle, with its succession of warring sky gods, closely parallels <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hesiod">Hesiod</a>'s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#theogony">Theogony</a></em>, one of the earliest documented channels of cultural transmission between the Near East and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#late-bronze-age-collapse"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age-collapse.jpg" alt="Late Bronze Age collapse" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#late-bronze-age-collapse">Late Bronze Age collapse</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bronze_Age_End.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Alexikoua, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Hittite Empire collapsed in the early twelfth century BCE as part of the broader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#late-bronze-age-collapse">Bronze Age Collapse</a>, which destroyed or crippled most of the major states of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> within a generation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sea-peoples">Sea Peoples</a>, migrating confederacies whose raids disrupted the coastal trade networks on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> international system depended, are traditionally cast as the immediate agents, though their exact role remains debated. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hattusa">Hattusa</a> was abandoned and burned around 1180 BCE under the last known king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#suppiluliuma-ii">Suppiluliuma II</a>, and never again served as a royal capital. Contributing factors included succession conflicts around the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tudhaliya-iv">Tudhaliya IV</a>, prolonged drought and famine documented in correspondence requesting emergency grain shipments from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, and the fragmentation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#vassal">vassal</a> network that had sustained imperial revenues. Assyrian expansion westward added pressure on the eastern frontier. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#neo-hittite-states">Neo-Hittite</a> city-states in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#syria">Syria</a> and south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#anatolia">Anatolia</a>, including Carchemish and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#malatya">Malatya</a>, preserved elements of Hittite political and artistic tradition for centuries before absorption into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>, representing the civilisation's epilogue rather than its continuation.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Hittite Empire's enduring significance lies in its demonstration that great-power competition could be managed through formal diplomacy as effectively as through military force, producing in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-kadesh">Treaty of Kadesh</a> a model of negotiated international order whose conceptual descendants remain foundational to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#international-law">international law</a> today.</em></p>

